Explain the role of the pulmonary artery in the circulatory system, including the type of blood it carries and its relationship with the heart.
The pulmonary artery plays a crucial role in the circulatory system by carrying deoxygenated blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs. It is an artery in the pulmonary circulation that facilitates the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the blood and the lungs. The pulmonary artery starts as the pulmonary trunk or main pulmonary artery, which begins at the base of the right ventricle of the heart. From there, it branches into smaller arteries, eventually leading to the capillaries that surround the pulmonary alveoli, where gas exchange occurs.
