What is the architectural style of the Great Mosque at Cordoba, and how does it relate to other notable Islamic architectural styles?
The Great Mosque of Córdoba was built in the Umayyad style of architecture, with variations inspired by indigenous Roman and Christian Visigothic structures. This style is characterized by the use of horseshoe arches, which were a major innovation in Islamic architecture. The Umayyad style, in turn, influenced the development of other notable Islamic architectural styles, such as the Abbasid mosques, T-type mosques, and central-dome mosques of Anatolia. The Mezquita in Córdoba is considered one of the four wonders of the medieval Islamic world and is a prime example of the evolution of Islamic architecture.
