What is the history of daylight saving time (DST) implementation in the United States, including the first year it was introduced and any subsequent changes or repeals?
The first implementation of daylight saving time (DST) in the United States was during World War I, when the Standard Time Act of 1918 was passed, which established DST from March 31 to October 31. However, it was repealed in 1919.

During World War II, the U.S. reinstated DST with the Uniform Time Act of 1942, which set DST to begin on the last Sunday in April and end on the last Sunday in October.

In 1966, the Uniform Time Act was amended to start DST on the last Sunday in January and end on the last Sunday in October.

The Energy Policy Act of 2005 extended the duration of DST by about four weeks, starting it on the second Sunday in March and ending it on the first Sunday in November.

It's worth noting that some states, such as Hawaii and Arizona (except for the Navajo Nation, which does observe DST), have opted out of observing DST.
