Describe the role and significance of the myelin sheath in the peripheral nervous system, including its impact on nerve function and transmission.
The myelin sheath in the peripheral nervous system plays a crucial role in facilitating fast and efficient transmission of nerve impulses. It is produced by Schwann cells and reduces membrane capacitance, increasing membrane resistance in the inter-node intervals. This allows for a saltatory movement of action potentials from node to node, enabling rapid nerve conduction. The myelin sheath acts as an insulator, reducing the leakage of electrical charge and allowing the action potential to jump from node to node, resulting in faster transmission of nerve impulses.
