What is the history of daylight saving time in the United States, including its initial implementation and any subsequent changes or repeals?
Daylight saving time in the United States was initially proposed by Benjamin Franklin in 1784, but it was not implemented until the Daylight Saving Act of 1917 was enacted by the Dominion of Newfoundland, which was followed by the United States in 1918 with the Standard Time Act of 1918. However, it was repealed in 1919. The practice was re-established nationally during World War II and has been observed continuously since then, with some variations among states. The Uniform Time Act of 1966 further established the system.
